 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why glue ear is a childhood problem",
   "html": "<p>The auditory tube opens into the nasopharynx right beside the adenoids. In children the tube is shorter, wider and more horizontal, and the adenoids are large, so infection and obstruction at that opening block ventilation of the middle ear. Fluid collects behind the drum and hearing drops. That is why adenoidectomy and grommets are common childhood operations, and why the lecture puts the tube opening and the pharyngeal tonsil on the same slide.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's nasopharynx features, read with the auditory tube's role as the middle ear connection.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the gap between the constrictors matters",
   "town": "",
   "html": "<p>The constrictors overlap like stacked flowerpots, but the overlap is not complete: a weak area, Killian's dehiscence, lies between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus parts of the inferior constrictor. If cricopharyngeus fails to relax properly in the third stage of swallowing, pressure pushes the mucosa through that gap and forms a pharyngeal pouch. The anatomy of the inferior constrictor's two parts is exactly what explains it.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's inferior constrictor table and its third stage of deglutition.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why a fishbone lodges in the piriform fossa",
   "html": "<p>The larynx bulges backwards into the laryngopharynx, leaving a gutter on each side. Food is meant to slide down these gutters around the closed laryngeal inlet, which is why they exist. A sharp fragment, though, can catch in the mucosa there instead of passing on. The lecture's clinical note is the reason the piriform fossa is the first place looked at when someone swallows a bone.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's piriform fossa slide and clinical note.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the gag reflex tests two nerves",
   "html": "<p>Touching the back of the pharynx tests sensation carried by CN IX and the motor response of the constrictors through CN X. That is why the gag reflex is used to assess the two nerves together, and why loss of it, along with a drooping palate, points to a lesion of the vagus or the pharyngeal plexus rather than to a local throat problem.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's innervation slides.</p>"
  }
 ],
